        <methods>
            <methodStep>
                <description>
                    <para>Besides the records from the main survey, an additional 25 records of opportunistically observed species were added before upload.</para>
                </description>
            </methodStep>
            <sampling>
                <studyExtent>
                    <description>
                        <para>The data contain detections of amphibians and reptiles recorded during time-constrained encounter surveys in six rainforest remnants on the western Valparai Plateau, Anamalai Hills, Tamil Nadu, India. The six remnants surveyed were Kalyanapandal-Pudukadu (KP), Candura (CAN), Murugalli Sholayar (MS), Murugalli Hospital (MH), Murugalli Black Bridge (BB), and Murugalli Sykes (SYK).</para>
                    </description>
                </studyExtent>
                <samplingDescription>
                    <para>Surveys were conducted as time-constrained encounter surveys by a single observer walking slowly through each rainforest remnant, visually searching for amphibians and reptiles in the leaf litter, on the ground, on vegetation, on rocks, and in and around water bodies. 
Surveys were carried out during September and October 2017, and included both diurnal and nocturnal surveys across the study sites. For each detection, species identity, individual count, microhabitat (e.g., leaf litter, grass, vegetation, stream, rock), altitude, and weather were recorded. Survey effort was measured in observer-hours. Encounter rates were computed as the number of specimens recorded per person-hour of survey effort. A total of 40 surveys of one hour each were done across the six fragments, with 4 to 12 surveys per fragment.

Further details of the study area and methods are available in Harikrishnan et al. (2018) </para>
                </samplingDescription>
            </sampling>
            <qualityControl>
                <description>
                    <para>Raw data were screened for gaps, and verbatim identification was matched to the correct scientific name of the taxon in worksheets. Geographic location was recorded at the level of each survey in each forest remnant with GPS accuracy set at 150 m to assign the coordinate uncertainty in metres. Notes and other details (day/night, weather, altitude, microhabitat) were concatenated into the habitat and occurrenceRemarks fields.</para>
                </description>
            </qualityControl>
        </methods>
